course: dictionary
lesson: 2479
title: Daydreaming
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Daydreaming means thinking about happy or imagined things while you are awake, so you stop noticing what is around you. Look at the words around it for clues. If a teacher says «stop daydreaming», they want you to pay attention again.
explain: «Daydreaming is normal and good for imagination, but it can make you miss instructions; writing them down or sitting nearer the front helps.» The clue is miss instructions. Your mind has wandered away from what you should be hearing. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: In stories, a character who is «caught daydreaming» is lost in thought or imagining. The words around it tell you whether daydreaming is a problem or a good thing, so always read the whole sentence.
fact: Daydreaming means thinking about happy or imagined things while you are awake.
fact: «Daydreaming» can also mean not paying attention, especially in class.
fact: Daydreaming can help your imagination and creativity.
fact: Writing instructions down or sitting nearer the front can help you stop daydreaming and catch what the teacher says.
fact: «Daydreaming» can be a noun or a verb: «daydreaming is normal» and «I was daydreaming».
exercise: «Daydreaming is normal and good for imagination, but it can make you miss instructions.» What can daydreaming make you do? | *Miss instructions | Run faster than your friends | Sleep through the night | Learn every word in the dictionary
exercise: Mrs Patel says, «Stop daydreaming, Ali, and listen.» What is she asking Ali to do? | Write a story about a dragon | Go to sleep at his desk | Imagine a faraway island | *Pay attention again
exercise: Which sentence shows daydreaming as a good thing? | Daydreaming is the same as listening carefully | Daydreaming means reading very fast | *Daydreaming helps my imagination grow | Daydreaming means I always know what the teacher said
test: In «Maya was caught daydreaming by the window», what does caught daydreaming suggest? | She was reading a book on the window ledge | *Her mind was far away from the lesson | She was counting the cars outside
test: In «The boy was daydreaming and missed the question», which phrase is the closest swap for daydreaming? | Shouting loudly | Fast asleep | *Lost in thought
exam: Why does the writer say sitting nearer the front helps? | It stops you from having ideas | *It helps you pay attention so you don't miss instructions | It makes the room warmer | It helps you grow taller
